{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 43, :comments ["<b>And behold, they had made, etc. — so they did.</b> The reason He repeated a second time <b>so they did</b>, is that His saying <b>and behold, they had made</b> draws upon what precedes: <b>and he saw… and behold, it was made</b> — meaning the substance of the deed itself. And the intention in this is that there had been no set measure of time, and he saw that behold, they had made it in a short time." "<b>Further</b>, it may intend in His saying <b>they made it</b> — in the sense of preparation (תיקון), that they prepared it as required. And further, He informs that he perceived insight in it — that they had made it just as HaShem had commanded in the details of its rule, and this is His saying <b>as</b>… <b>so they did.</b>" "<b>Further</b>, it may intend according to their words, of blessed memory (Zevachim 62a), that there are details of the commandments that are not disqualifying but are merely for the mitzvah, and the Sages enumerated the things that disqualify one another. And the verse informs through the doubling of the deed to say that they did everything that He commanded, even things that are merely for a mitzvah, of the choicest kind." "<b>And Moshe blessed them.</b> The reason it was necessary to say <b>Moshe</b> and not rely on the antecedent pronoun, is to say: let this blessing not be light in your eyes, for Moshe, the man of Elohim, blessed them. And a great thing the verse informs through this matter. And according to this reason it is that the verse informed and said <b>and he blessed them</b> — for aside from its being Moshe, the Torah does not tell whether one man blesses his fellow."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 39, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:37:25.294048593Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}